[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"JWT (JSON Web Tokens): Using JWT for Stateless Authentication in Spring Boot"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"JSON Web Token (JWT) is an open standard (RFC 7519) that defines a compact and self-contained way for securely transmitting information between parties as a JSON object. This information can be verified and trusted because it is digitally signed. JWTs can be signed using a secret (with the HMAC algorithm) or a public/private key pair using RSA or ECDSA. In the context of Spring Boot, JWTs are commonly used for implementing stateless authentication and authorization."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Stateless Authentication with JWT"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Traditional session-based authentication requires the server to maintain session state for each user. This can lead to scalability issues. JWTs, on the other hand, allow for stateless authentication. The server doesn't need to store any session information. Each request from the client includes the JWT, which the server verifies to authenticate the user."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Java Development Kit (JDK) 8 or later"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Maven or Gradle"},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Spring Boot (2.x or 3.x)"},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Basic understanding of Spring Security"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Project Setup"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Create a new Spring Boot project using Spring Initializr (start.spring.io). Include the following dependencies:"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Spring Web"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Spring Security"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"jjwt-api, jjwt-impl, jjwt-jackson (for JWT support)"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here's an example of the `pom.xml` (Maven) file:"},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
	xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
